WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.650
Researchers put a few black and white

2
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.570
stickers on a real stop sign.

3
00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.050
A road-sign classifier then read it as a

4
00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.390
speed limit sign in most test photos.

5
00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.950
A person sees a stop sign.

6
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:14.570
The model does not.

7
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.300
Today: why tiny, targeted changes fool

8
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.980
accurate classifiers, how the fast

9
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.700
gradient sign method builds them, and how

10
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.980
to teach students to evaluate defences

11
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:27.340
honestly.

12
00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.370
An adversarial example is an input

13
00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.690
changed slightly on purpose so that

14
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:34.710
a model gets it wrong.

15
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.560
The change is chosen by following the

16
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.480
model's own gradient, so it is small

17
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.640
for us but large for the model.

18
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.280
The fast gradient sign method moves

19
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.020
every input feature by epsilon in the

20
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.000
direction that increases the loss.

21
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.540
For a linear score, the change adds up

22
00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.500
across thousands of features, which is why

23
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.900
high-dimensional models are so easy to

24
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:00.160
push.

25
00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:04.470
Take weights two, minus one and

26
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.030
three, and epsilon one tenth.

27
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.180
Each feature moves by one tenth

28
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.780
against its weight's sign.

29
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.080
The score drops by one tenth times

30
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.180
six, which is six tenths, enough to

31
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.680
flip a borderline prediction.

32
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.820
The strongest common defence

33
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.680
is adversarial training.

34
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.400
During training the model sees

35
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.940
attacked versions of each example and

36
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.380
learns to classify them correctly.

37
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.870
It costs extra computation and

38
00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.650
usually some clean accuracy.

39
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.000
Students often claim a defence works

40
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.320
because their own attack failed.

41
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.920
Many published defences were later

42
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.200
broken by attacks designed for them.

43
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.160
Teach them to test every defence with an

44
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.140
adaptive attack that knows how it works.

45
00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:58.980
Quick check!

46
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.160
A linear model has weights

47
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.360
two, minus one and three.

48
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.720
With the fast gradient sign

49
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:09.260
method and epsilon one tenth, how

50
00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.280
much can the score change?

51
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.820
Six tenths.

52
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.160
Epsilon times the sum of the absolute

53
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.880
weights: one tenth times six.

54
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.770
In the app, open the adversarial lab.

55
00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.470
Raise epsilon step by step, watch the

56
00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.030
prediction flip, and notice how small

57
00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:29.990
the change looks to you.

58
00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:31.860
Your turn.

59
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.540
A student's defence stops the

60
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.640
fast gradient sign method.

61
00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.470
Which test should come next

62
00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.390
before claiming it is robust?

63
00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.720
An adaptive attack built for that defence.

64
00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.710
Remember: adversarial examples follow the

65
00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:53.930
gradient, small changes add up over many

66
00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.510
features, and a defence is only as good as

67
00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.150
the strongest attack it survives.

68
00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.570
Try it in your next lecture: give pairs a

69
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.950
tiny linear classifier on paper.

70
00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.910
One student attacks it with a fixed

71
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.750
epsilon, the other proposes a defence.

72
00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.910
Then they swap and attack the defence.

73
00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.600
Finish by comparing which

74
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:18.720
defences survived.
